## ABSTRACT

This dissertation provides the mathematical and architectural foundations of the Global Asteroid Defense Infrastructure (GADI) Version 6.0, a lean-resilience coordination framework for near-Earth object impact prevention. We derive activation thresholds from Palermo-scale dynamics and Bayesian false-positive control, formulate a multi-sensor cryptographic consensus protocol resistant to spoofing, model the two-speed decision logic as a time-optimal control problem, optimize mission profile selection under uncertainty via stochastic programming, and analyze financial pre-commitment enforcement through game theory. The framework is validated through Monte Carlo stress testing across warning horizons from 24 months to 6 months, demonstrating that institutional latency is eliminated as a catastrophic amplifier. All technical specifications—sensor network redundancy, escrow structure, nuclear pathway trigger logic, AI advisory boundaries, and risk-equity allocation—are explicitly parameterized. The dissertation establishes that a conditional, time-calibrated coordination architecture can guarantee deflection response without permanent supranational authority, provided it respects the physics of orbital mechanics and the hard constraints of sovereign decision-making.

