## CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND PROBLEM FORMULATION

The deflection of a hazardous near-Earth object (NEO) is an optimal control problem with a shrinking action space. Let \( t_d \) be the time of detection, \( t_i \) the predicted impact epoch, and \( \Delta v \) the required velocity change to achieve a safe miss distance. The available response time \( T = t_i - t_d \) determines the feasible set of deflection missions. As \( T \) decreases, the minimum achievable \( \Delta v \) grows nonlinearly, while the set of political actors required for authorization remains constant. The central institutional failure is that decision latency \( \tau_d \) consumes a significant fraction of \( T \), transforming a controllable threat into an unavoidable catastrophe.

This dissertation solves the coordination design problem: given a stochastic threat arrival process, an ensemble of sovereign actors with heterogeneous capabilities, and a time-varying set of admissible deflection actions \( \mathcal{A}(t) \), construct a conditional commitment architecture that minimizes expected decision latency while satisfying constitutional, legal, and political constraints.

