## CHAPTER 3: ARCHITECTURE AND CONTROL THEORY OF THE LEAN-RESILIENCE FRAMEWORK

### 3.1 State Machine and Phase Transitions

The protocol is a finite state machine with states: Standby (S), Verification (V), Reconnaissance (R), Decision (D), Execution (E), Sunset (X). Transitions are gated by physical conditions and timeouts. Let \( t \) be time since MRTC issuance. The decision epoch \( t_D \) is bounded by:

\[
t_D \leq 
\begin{cases}
60 \text{ days} & T \geq 2 \text{ years} \\
14 \text{ days} & T < 2 \text{ years} \\
7 \text{ days} & T < 12 \text{ months (Provisional Gate)}
\end{cases}
\]

The provisional execution gate (Tier C) allows the Mission Authority Node (MAN) to issue pre-positioning commands if \( T < 12 \) months post-recon. This is formalized as a control law:

\[
\mathbf{u}(t) = \mathbf{u}_{\text{pre}} \quad \text{if } T - t < \tau_{\text{ratify}}
\]

where \( \tau_{\text{ratify}} = 30 \) days is the retroactive ratification window. The action is valid provided the subsequent Core vote achieves 75% approval; otherwise, liability protection applies.

### 3.2 Optimal Decision Threshold Design

We pose the activation threshold selection as a multi-objective optimization minimizing expected decision latency \( \mathbb{E}[\tau_D] \) and false-positive probability \( \alpha \), subject to a true-positive probability \( 1 - \beta \geq 0.99 \). Using a Pareto analysis, the dual-threshold \( \mathcal{P} \geq -1.0 \) coupled with \( P_{\text{imp}} \geq 1\% \) dominates single-threshold policies across all warning times.

The 75% supermajority for standard tier is derived from a voting game where each Core Participant has a bond-weighted vote. The quota \( q = 0.75 \) ensures no single actor can veto the mission, but two large actors must cooperate, aligning with the dual-state requirement for nuclear profiles.

### 3.3 Escrow and Penalty Functions

The bond structure is a commitment device. Let \( B_1, B_2 \) be the Phase 1 and Phase 2 bonds. The utility of a Core Participant \( i \) if defecting is:

\[
U_i(\text{defect}) = R_i - p_{\text{imp}} L_i - \lambda B
\]

where \( R_i \) is political rent from non-participation, \( L_i \) the expected loss from impact, and \( \lambda \in \{0.5, 1\} \) the forfeiture fraction. The framework sets \( B_2 = \$6 \text{B} \) such that \( \lambda B > R_i \) for reasonable political calculations, ensuring defection is dominated when \( p_{\text{imp}} \) is non-negligible. The full game analysis, including incomplete information and reputation effects, confirms that the trembling-hand perfect equilibrium is full compliance under high threat levels.

