## CHAPTER 5: VERIFICATION AND CRYPTOGRAPHIC CONSENSUS MODELING

### 5.1 MRTC Signature Aggregation

Each sensor network \( i \) computes its state estimate \( \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i \) with covariance \( \mathbf{P}_i \). The consensus condition uses a generalized distance:

\[
d_{ij} = (\hat{\mathbf{x}}_i - \hat{\mathbf{x}}_j)^\top (\mathbf{P}_i + \mathbf{P}_j)^{-1} (\hat{\mathbf{x}}_i - \hat{\mathbf{x}}_j) \leq \chi^2_{6,0.99}
\]

A BFT agreement is achieved if a quorum of 3 networks satisfies pairwise consistency. The physical observation requirement adds a confirmation signature \( \sigma_p \) that cannot be generated without access to a radar or separated optical facility, thus raising the spoofing attack cost to physical compromise.

### 5.2 False-Positive and False-Negative Trade-off

The overall false-positive probability of the verification layer is:

\[
\alpha_{\text{system}} = \alpha_{\text{threshold}} \times P(\text{spoof} | \text{no threat}) + \alpha_{\text{BFT}} 
\]

With \( \alpha_{\text{threshold}} = 10^{-4} \) and BFT protocol tolerance of one faulty node, \( \alpha_{\text{BFT}} < 10^{-5} \). The false-negative probability \( \beta \) is determined by the likelihood that a real threat fails consensus due to sensor failure or attack. Redundant networks (N=5, f=1) give \( \beta \approx 10^{-3} \), acceptable for existential risk.

