## CHAPTER 6: FINANCIAL PRE-COMMITMENT AND GAME-THEORETIC ENFORCEMENT

### 6.1 Bond Valuation and Escrow Dynamics

The escrow fund \( S \) is composed of assets with liquidity \( \ell \). The amount \( B_2 = 6 \times 10^9 \) USD per Core Participant is sized such that the liquidation shock during crisis does not exceed 5% of the total sovereign bond market in the relevant maturity. We model the bond market impact using a Kyle model, confirming that the 40% cash buffer absorbs immediate needs while AAA bonds can be liquidated gradually.

### 6.2 Evolutionary Game Model of Compliance

Consider a population of states with types: compliant (C) and defector (D). The payoff matrix is:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & C & D \\
\hline
C & ( -c + s, -c + s ) & ( -c - \lambda B, 0 ) \\
D & ( 0, -c - \lambda B ) & ( -L p_{\text{imp}}, -L p_{\text{imp}} )
\end{array}
\]

where \( c \) is the cost of contributing, \( s \) is the shared survival benefit, \( L \) the loss, and \( \lambda B \) the forfeited bond. The replicator dynamics show that for \( p_{\text{imp}} > c / (L + \lambda B) \), compliance is an evolutionarily stable strategy. With \( c \approx 10^8 \) /year, \( L \approx 10^{13} \), \( \lambda B \approx 6 \times 10^9 \), the threshold \( p_{\text{imp}} \approx 10^{-5} \) is far below the activation threshold, guaranteeing that in any activated scenario, defection is maladaptive.

