We have shown that the coordination problem of planetary defense can be reduced to a set of computable thresholds, optimal control laws, cryptographic consensus rules, and game-theoretic incentive structures. GADI v6.0 embodies these in a conditional, time-calibrated architecture that minimizes expected decision latency subject to democratic constraints. The mathematical rigor confirms that effective deflection governance does not require global government; it requires carefully designed pre-commitment, tiered activation, and honest legal parameterization. The framework is now ready for diplomatic piloting, capability auditing, and the first live exercise at Apophis 2029—where mathematics will meet metal, and we will test whether humanity can indeed coordinate to save itself.

