**ANNEX N: GAME‑THEORETIC AND FINANCIAL MODELING DETAILS**

**N.1 Compliance as Evolutionary Stable Strategy**
The payoff matrix (see Chapter 6) yields replicator dynamics:
\[
\dot{x}_C = x_C(1-x_C)\big[ (-c + s) - ( -L\,p_{\text{imp}} ) \big]
\]
where \(x_C\) is the fraction of compliant Cores. With \(c \approx 10^8\) $/yr, \(L \approx 10^{13}\) $, \(p_{\text{imp}} \ge 0.01\) (activation threshold), the compliant strategy dominates. Even with 20% defector probability, \(P_{\text{success}} > 0.85\) for > 12 months warning.

**N.2 Bond Market Stability Under Escrow Liquidation**
A $6 B withdrawal per Core (up to 4 concurrently) is modelled using a Kyle market impact model. The 40% cash buffer allows $9.6 B in immediate liquidity without any bond sales. The remaining sovereign bonds can be liquidated over 60 days, causing an estimated yield increase of < 3 bp on the affected issues—well within market absorption capacity. Irrevocable letters of credit and gold provide additional layers.

**N.3 Reputational Enforcement**
Public attribution and insurance‑market sanctions increase defection costs. The model shows that a combination of bond forfeiture, data exclusion, and commercial insurance premium surcharge (20% for 5 years) raises the effective cost of defection above $7 B, aligning incentives.

